WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.040
A nucleus has no membranes inside it.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.960
Yet each chromosome keeps to its own region.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.540
Ribosomes are assembled in one place, and splicing factors gather in others.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.700
So what holds things apart when there are no walls?

5
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:25.520
One proposal is that some of these bodies form by phase separation.

6
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.860
That idea makes predictions you can test.

7
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:34.073
A bright spot under the microscope can arise in three ways.

8
00:00:34.693 --> 00:00:40.593
In phase separation, molecules demix from their surroundings, as oil separates from

9
00:00:40.593 --> 00:00:47.393
vinegar. Or a protein can bind clustered sites, many copies of one sequence in one

10
00:00:47.393 --> 00:00:53.513
place. Or it can bridge chromatin, so the fibre collapses into a dense globule.

11
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:55.913
All three make a bright spot.

12
00:00:55.913 --> 00:00:59.173
They differ when you change the amount of protein.

13
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.947
Phase separation predicts a threshold.

14
00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:06.987
Below the saturation concentration, no bodies form.

15
00:01:07.987 --> 00:01:12.107
Above it, extra protein goes into the bodies, and the bodies grow.

16
00:01:12.847 --> 00:01:15.867
The concentration outside stays at the threshold.

17
00:01:16.587 --> 00:01:18.647
This is a buffered dilute phase.

18
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.290
Now put in numbers.

19
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.270
These data are made up, for illustration.

20
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:31.170
Assume one dense phase at 50 µM and a saturation concentration of 1

21
00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.670
µM. A simple mass balance gives the fraction of nuclear volume that the

22
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.910
dense phase takes up.

23
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:47.810
At totals of 2, 4, and 8 µM, bodies fill 2.0, 6.1, then 14.3 percent of

24
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:54.430
the nucleus. The bodies grow sevenfold, while the nucleoplasm stays near 1 µM.

25
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.830
Now test two proteins.

26
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:04.910
For protein A, the nucleoplasm measures 1.0, 1.0, then 1.1 µM.

27
00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:12.750
That fits. For protein B, it measures 1.5, 2.8, then 5.9.

28
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.530
Divide by the totals and you get 0.75, 0.70, then 0.74.

29
00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.490
Protein B keeps a roughly constant fraction outside the bodies.

30
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.390
That fits binding sites better.

31
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:34.530
Round shapes, fusion, fluorescence recovery, and hexanediol cannot tell these models

32
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.090
apart. Treat the result with care.

33
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:43.050
A protein that fails may still phase separate, as one part of a mixture.

34
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.850
A protein that passes has not been shown to need its body.

35
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.530
So change the amount of protein, and measure inside and outside the bodies.
